    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to the requirements for a\nlicense as a professional land surveyor; and providing for the repeal of\ncertain provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6316",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6316-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §7206-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6316\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to the\nrequirements for a license as a professional land surveyor; and provid-\ning for the repeal of certain provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to update certain licensing requirements for\nland surveyors.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends section 7206-a of the education law to update the\neducation and experience requirements for licensure for professional and\nsurveyors. The new update: allows for practical experience satisfactory\nto the State Education Department (SED); modifies the fee schedule for\ninitial licensure and the triennial registration period; allows appli-\ncants with an associate's degree in land surveying, or an associate's\ndegree or higher in engineering, math, or related science with credits\nin land surveying acceptable to SED to apply for a license in lieu of\nmeeting the education requirements currently specified in law; requires\napplicants to fulfill the education, experience, and examination\nrequirements in accordance with the Commissioner's regulations and\nprovides that upon the successful completion of the examination, an\nidentification card as an intern land surveyor be issued by SED; and\nrepeals in eight years, the pathway that allows individuals to be\nlicensed with eight years of practical experience in work satisfactory\nto SED.\n\nSection 2. Provides that this act shall take effect eighteen months\nafter it shall have become a law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHistorically, more land surveyors were licensed through an all experi-\nence route than through a combination of education and experience.  The\nnational Land Surveying licensure exams have been updated to a bachelor\nlevel of knowledge. Only 7 of the 51 US jurisdictions currently report\nhaving an all-experience route for licensure of land surveyors. There\nare currently 6 NYS educational programs with surveying programs/courses\nlocated throughout the New York State.\n\nIndividuals with an all-experience pathway are more likely to fail the\nlicensing exam on multiple occasions and the field has reported that\nthey are less likely to be hired once licensed. While this was common\npractice for many years in many states, including New York, the all\nexperience route to licensure is being repealed in a number of jurisdic-\ntions. This bill would phase-out the all experience route for anyone\nembarking on the path of licensure as a land surveyor.  However, those\nindividuals who are currently gaining experience to become licensed as a\nland surveyor via the all experience route would be able to continue\nthat path.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.7969, A.7969-A\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect eighteen months after it shall have become a\nlaw.\n",
